<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Modal Verbs - Grammar rules</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>Form</h2>
    <p>Use the base form of the next verb, with no <em>-s</em>, <em>-ed</em> or <em>-ing</em>. She might <strong>go</strong>. He should <strong>listen</strong>.</p>
    <p>She might <strong>goes</strong>. <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span> He should <strong>listens</strong>. <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span></p>
    <p>Do not add <em>-s</em> to the modal. He <strong>can</strong> run. <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span> He <strong>cans</strong> run. <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span></p>
    <p>Put <em>not</em> after the modal. She <strong>might not come</strong>. You <strong>must not enter</strong>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-pink">
    <h2>Must not, or do not have to</h2>
    <p>“You <strong>must not</strong> go.” Going is forbidden.</p>
    <p>“You <strong>do not have to</strong> go.” Going is not necessary. It is optional.</p>
    <p>These are different. Do not swap them.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
    <h2>Might or must?</h2>
    <p>“The lights are off and no one answers the door. They <strong>might</strong> be away.” Being away is one possible explanation.</p>
    <p>“They <strong>must</strong> be away” is a stronger inference: the speaker thinks they are almost certainly away. There could be another explanation, such as everyone being asleep or out in the garden. A strong inference is a conclusion from clues. It is not a rule or an obligation.</p>
  </section>
</div>
